[Music]  today was the day that it became very  clear the Fed will do whatever it can to  keep the economy in the markets  functioning the Fed rolled out two new  lending facilities for the first time  lending directly to big US companies and  it also brought back some facilities we  haven't seen since 2008 so when the Fed  wants in a pinch like this to get money  out into the economy it starts by buying  the safest assets out there buys its own  debt back US Treasury bonds so they  started last week buying Treasury  another government debt and then on  Friday they said they would start by a  municipal bond and what you're starting  to see now is that that's not enough so  the Fed is going really a step down to  slightly more risky assets which is  corporate debt though they're gonna buy  corporate bonds it's gonna ship  corporate loans and that's a really  unprecedented step the markets did not  respond well at all the Dow and the SP  stock indexes end-of-the-day down about  three percent  they're now off 30 percent or so from  from their highs just a month or two ago  the market had its best day since 1933  today and that was due to lawmakers  getting close to signing a massive  stimulus bill to help the economy the  Dow finished up 11% today and the S&amp;P  500 added nine point four percent which  marked its best days since 2008 markets  have actually been very volatile the  last couple of weeks and it's not been  unusual to see these really big down  days followed immediately after by  really big up days for instance even  though we had the biggest gain for the  Dow since 1933 today we've also within  the last couple of weeks seen some of  the stock market's worst Falls in  history on March 9th March 12 and March  16th  once there was another really volatile  day in the stock market the S&amp;P 500 rose  as much as 5% in the afternoon before  ending the day up 1.2 percent so is  another crazy day for investors and all  of us financial journalists watching at  home stocks closed positive for two days  in a row that's the first time that's  happened since February 12 Wednesday's  moves came with traders and investors  really trekking on Congress every five  minutes or so to see where the economic  stimulus package stands so markets rose  a lot on anticipation that it would pass  and they paired some of the gains on  signs that maybe it won't be as smooth  one of the most fascinating things about  Wednesday's moves was the massive slings  and airline stocks and Boeing Boeing  which is a big component of the Dow rose  24% and that's the positive side for  some people who track short-term gains  but bigger picture some investors are  still really worried about the intraday  swings and how big they are we're also  waiting to see again hard economic data  what's been really crazy is how fast  this has all happened in only a few  weeks we actually haven't seen much hard  data yet showing the really big slowdown  we're all preparing for as those data  points start coming in whether they're  worse than expected or somewhat work  better than feared things like that  could really shape how markets swing  over the coming days and then the next  week - today we saw that jobless claims  hit a record high the number of people  applying for unemployment benefits hit a  record 3.3 million so that's a sign of  how this coronavirus is taking a toll on  on humans and the economy it's really  hard to overstate how bad this number  was you know this was above the numbers  that we saw during the global financial  crisis and it's nearly five times the  previous high that we've ever  so there's this huge disconnect out  there you've got these really really  dark figures on jobless claims the worst  that we've ever seen and on the other  hand you have stocks soaring they just  ripped higher the Dow Jones Industrial  Average was up six percent and it just  logged its best in three days streak  since the 1930s some people think that  we can actually have a pretty speedy  recovery in the third and fourth  quarters of this year and other people  think that hey we don't know we could be  in for prolonged downturn so stocks  ended Friday down but three days of  gains despite you know some pretty  encouraging signs out of Washington  where the big coronavirus eight bill did  get passed and signed by the president  so stocks had a tough day that said  there were some more encouraging signs  out of the credit markets in particular  demand for this emergency funding that  the Federal Reserve has been providing  overnight dramatically fell it's this  very crucial part of the funding markets  and it literally keeps lights on  overnight at banks and companies you  know all around America two weeks ago  when the Fed rolled out its repo  facility it had more than 250 billion  dollars of demand people in the market  saying I knew cash today had only just a  little bit above 10 billion and so that  tells you that the private market is  starting to do its job again and maybe  the Fed isn't needed quite so much there  one thing that's important to remember  is this is at the end of the day a  health crisis and the bill that comes  through Congress certainly has has about  a hundred and fifty billion dollars for  hospitals it has aid for municipalities  but it can't it can't fight the virus on  its own and so markets are still gonna  be pretty pretty concerned until there's  better data on the coronavirus front

